damaged_code:
```rust
// A chain of references that couples struct lifetimes all the way up,
// adopted to avoid cloning a short string that was never measured as a cost
struct FileRef<'a> { content: &'a str }
struct Parser<'a> { file: FileRef<'a> }
struct Compiler<'a> { parser: Parser<'a> }

struct User<'a> { name: &'a str }
struct Session<'a> { user: User<'a> }
```

counterexample:
Highly specialized local zero-copy parsers and borrowed views intentionally bounded by their input lifetime are legitimate, especially for measured performance needs; scoped threads and sufficiently long-lived input can also make borrowed models workable. An already-owned model is outside scope.
```rust
struct User {
    name: String, // Clean and owned
}
```

fix:
Move owned `String`/`Vec<u8>` values into long-lived models when ownership is required; clone only where independent ownership actually requires it. Use `Arc<str>`/`Arc<T>` for shared ownership when appropriate (noting `Send`/`Sync` requirements for cross-thread use), or `Cow` for genuinely useful borrowed-or-owned APIs; `Cow` with a borrowed variant still carries lifetime constraints and is not automatically a cure for lifetime propagation.
```rust
struct FileData { content: String }
struct Parser { file: FileData }
struct Compiler { parser: Parser }
```
